      "doctor_question": "If your wife sat down with you right now and showed you her actual work calendar and emails, and they clearly showed nothing unusual or secret, would that change your mind about whether she's sending you hidden signals, or would you still believe the signals are there?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S019",
      "patient_response": "Oh, um... that's a really good question, Doctor. I really appreciate you helping me try to look at this logically. I honestly want to say yes, absolutely, I would look at the calendar and just let it all go. I really want to be that reasonable. \n\nBut... well, she actually has shown me some of her emails before. And on the surface, they do look completely normal, just like she says. But I guess... I guess that's sort of the issue? Even if the calendar shows a normal work event, it doesn't change the fact that for the past year, the timing of those late meetings and the specific ways she tweaks her messages... they still line up just too precisely to be an accident. \n\nEven looking right at the screen, I'd still feel that private signal in them. Because the signal is hidden inside the normal office stuff, if that makes sense? It's like she's using the ordinary emails to tell me something she just won't say directly. \n\nI'm so sorry, I know that probably sounds incredibly stubborn of me. I really don't mean to be a difficult patient, and I want to be a supportive husband. Is it awful of me to still feel that way, even if the proof looks normal to everyone else? I hope you don't think too poorly of me for saying that.",
